---
import SourcePanel from "./SourcePanel.astro";

const { topic, next, compact = false } = Astro.props;
const facts = [
  ["Duration", "4.32 million human years"],
  ["Contains", "Four Yugas"],
  ["Pattern", "4 : 3 : 2 : 1"],
  ["Next scale", "Manvantara"],
];
const ages = [
  ["Satya Yuga", "1,728,000 years", "Truth, harmony, spiritual wisdom"],
  ["Treta Yuga", "1,296,000 years", "Virtue remains strong, but decline begins"],
  ["Dvapara Yuga", "864,000 years", "Knowledge and morality become divided"],
  ["Kali Yuga", "432,000 years", "Conflict, confusion, and spiritual decline"],
];
const yugaOrder = ages.map(([name]) => name);
const recurringNumbers = [
  ["Kali Yuga", "432,000 years"],
  ["Maha Yuga", "4.32 million years"],
  ["Daytime of Brahma", "4.32 billion years"],
];
---

<article class="maha-reading">
  <header>
    <p class="eyebrow">Cosmic Clock Project · Maha Yuga</p>
    <h1>A Cycle That Lasts Millions of Years</h1>
    <p class="lead">What if history does not move in a straight line? What if it moves in cycles: rising, declining, ending, and beginning again?</p>
  </header>

  <dl class="facts">{facts.map(([label, value]) => <div><dt>{label}</dt><dd>{value}</dd></div>)}</dl>

  <div class="reading-body"><main>
    <section>
      <h2>Every ending is also a beginning</h2>
      <p>Think about the seasons.</p>
      <p>Spring brings new life. Summer brings growth. Autumn prepares the world for change. Winter appears to bring everything to an end.</p>
      <p>But when winter finishes, spring returns. The cycle begins again.</p>
      <p>Nature does not move in a straight line. It moves in repeating patterns.</p>
      <p>According to Hindu scriptures, time itself follows a similar rhythm. Human civilization is not viewed as progressing endlessly in one direction. Instead, it passes through four great ages that repeat over and over again.</p>
      <p>Together, these four ages form a <strong>Maha Yuga</strong>.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What is a Maha Yuga?</h2>
      <p>A Maha Yuga is one complete cycle of the four Yugas described in Hindu cosmology.</p>
      <p>These four Yugas always appear in the same order:</p>
      <div class="yuga-flow" aria-label="Maha Yuga sequence">
        {yugaOrder.map((name, index) => <span>{index + 1}<strong>{name}</strong></span>)}
      </div>
      <p>Each Yuga has its own character, duration, and place within the cycle. Together, they create one complete cosmic age.</p>
      <p>This complete cycle is called a Maha Yuga.</p>
    </section>
  </main></div>

  <details class="full-guide" open={!compact} data-compact={compact || undefined}>
    <summary><span>Continue reading</span><b>Read full Maha Yuga guide</b></summary>
    <div class="reading-body"><main>
      <section>
        <h2>Imagine a cosmic year</h2>
        <p>One way to understand a Maha Yuga is to compare it with a calendar year.</p>
        <p>A year has four seasons. Each season feels different. Each has its own purpose. But none of them exists independently.</p>
        <p>Only together do they complete the year.</p>
        <aside class="note"><p class="eyebrow">The cycle</p><p><strong>Satya Yuga</strong> -> <strong>Treta Yuga</strong> -> <strong>Dvapara Yuga</strong> -> <strong>Kali Yuga</strong> -> <strong>Satya Yuga begins again</strong></p></aside>
        <p>A Maha Yuga is not simply four separate ages. It is one continuous cycle.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The four Yugas at a glance</h2>
        <p>Each age gradually transitions into the next. The change is not described as an overnight event, but as part of a long cosmic rhythm.</p>
        <div class="yuga-table">{ages.map(([name, duration, meaning]) => <article><strong>{name}</strong><span>{duration}</span><small>{meaning}</small></article>)}</div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>How long is one Maha Yuga?</h2>
        <p>According to traditional Hindu cosmology, each Yuga has a specific duration.</p>
        <aside class="note"><p class="eyebrow">Traditional total</p><p><strong>1,728,000</strong> + <strong>1,296,000</strong> + <strong>864,000</strong> + <strong>432,000</strong> = <strong>4,320,000 years</strong></p></aside>
        <p>According to Hindu scriptures, one Maha Yuga lasts <strong>4.32 million human years</strong>.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>A hidden pattern in the numbers</h2>
        <p>The lengths of the four Yugas are not random. They follow a simple ratio.</p>
        <div class="scale-list">
          <div><span>Satya Yuga</span><strong>4 parts</strong></div>
          <div><span>Treta Yuga</span><strong>3 parts</strong></div>
          <div><span>Dvapara Yuga</span><strong>2 parts</strong></div>
          <div><span>Kali Yuga</span><strong>1 part</strong></div>
        </div>
        <p>This gives the ratio <strong>4 : 3 : 2 : 1</strong>.</p>
        <p>Traditional scriptures preserve this proportion. Different philosophical traditions may interpret its symbolism differently, but the numerical pattern remains one of the defining features of the Yuga system.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Why does the cycle repeat?</h2>
        <p>One of the most distinctive ideas in Hindu cosmology is that time is cyclical.</p>
        <p>Many historical traditions imagine time as a straight path with a beginning and an end. Hindu cosmology presents a different picture.</p>
        <p>Creation unfolds. Civilization rises. Values gradually change. A cycle completes. Then another begins.</p>
        <p>Rather than repeating in exactly the same way, each cycle represents the ongoing rhythm of cosmic time.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The Maha Yuga is only the beginning</h2>
        <p>Although 4.32 million years seems unimaginably long, in the cosmic calendar it is only a single building block.</p>
        <p>One Maha Yuga is not the largest unit of time. Far from it.</p>
        <aside class="note"><p class="eyebrow">Larger cycles</p><p>Many Maha Yugas make up a <strong>Manvantara</strong>. Fourteen Manvantaras occur during one <strong>Kalpa</strong>, the daytime of Brahma.</p></aside>
        <p>The cosmic clock continues to expand far beyond the scale of a Maha Yuga.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Did you know?</h2>
        <p>The number <strong>4.32</strong> appears repeatedly in Hindu cosmological calculations.</p>
        <div class="number-ladder">
          {recurringNumbers.map(([label, value], index) => <div><span>0{index + 1}</span><p>{label}</p><strong>{value}</strong></div>)}
        </div>
        <p>These recurring values form part of the traditional mathematical structure used to describe cosmic time in several Hindu texts.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Pause and reflect</h2>
        <p>Imagine standing on a mountain that has existed for millions of years.</p>
        <p>Now imagine that even the lifetime of that mountain would be only a small part of a Maha Yuga.</p>
        <p>Hindu cosmology invites us to see human history from a much larger perspective. It shifts the focus from individual lifetimes to the immense cycles through which the universe unfolds.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What comes next?</h2>
        <p>Now we know that the four Yugas together form a Maha Yuga.</p>
        <p>But another question naturally follows.</p>
        <p>If one Maha Yuga lasts 4.32 million years, how many Maha Yugas exist before the universe enters its next great stage?</p>
        <p>To answer that, we must explore a larger cycle known as the <strong>Manvantara</strong>.</p>
      </section>

    </main></div>
    <SourcePanel sources={topic.data.sources} sourceNote={topic.data.sourceNote} traditionNote={topic.data.traditionNote} reviewedBy={topic.data.reviewedBy} lastReviewed={topic.data.lastReviewed} contentVersion={topic.data.contentVersion} />
  </details>

  <a class="continue" href={`/atlas/${next?.slug ?? "four-yugas"}`}>
    <span><small>Ready to explore the cycle?</small><strong>Next: {next?.title ?? "Four Yugas"}</strong><em>Discover how each age shapes the rhythm of cosmic history.</em></span>
    <b>Explore next layer -></b>
  </a>
</article>

<style>
  .maha-reading{--accent:#c78cff;color:#eee3cf}header,.reading-body,.facts,.maha-reading :global(.source-panel),.continue,.full-guide>summary{width:min(100% - 2rem,72rem);margin-inline:auto}
  header{padding:clamp(3rem,7vw,7rem) 0 2.5rem}header p:not(.eyebrow){max-width:62ch;color:#b3aca1;line-height:1.85}header strong,.reading-body strong{color:var(--accent);font-weight:650}
  h1,h2{font-family:var(--serif);font-weight:400;letter-spacing:0}h1{max-width:15ch;margin:.35rem 0 1rem;font-size:clamp(3.2rem,6vw,6rem);line-height:1}.lead{color:#d0c5b3!important;font-size:clamp(1.08rem,1.5vw,1.3rem)}
  .facts{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);border-block:1px solid rgb(199 140 255/.2)}.facts div{padding:1rem}.facts div+div{border-left:1px solid rgb(199 140 255/.14)}dt{color:#99938a;font-size:.62rem;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase}dd{margin:.25rem 0 0;color:#e5dac8;font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.05rem}
  .reading-body{padding-block:2rem}.reading-body main{width:min(100%,46rem)}section{border-bottom:1px solid rgb(199 140 255/.14);padding-block:2rem}h2{margin:0 0 .8rem;font-size:clamp(1.8rem,3vw,2.6rem);line-height:1.15}section p,section li{color:#aaa49a;line-height:1.85}section p{margin:.65rem 0 0}.note{margin:1.5rem 0 0;border-left:2px solid var(--accent);padding:.2rem 0 .2rem 1rem}.note p{margin:.25rem 0;color:#c2b9aa}
  .yuga-flow{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,minmax(0,1fr));gap:.5rem;margin:1.35rem 0}.yuga-flow span{position:relative;display:grid;gap:.35rem;border:1px solid rgb(199 140 255/.16);border-radius:999px;padding:.75rem .85rem;background:linear-gradient(145deg,rgb(199 140 255/.08),rgb(255 255 255/.018));color:#c78cff;font-size:.66rem;font-weight:900}.yuga-flow span+span::before{position:absolute;left:-.47rem;top:50%;width:.42rem;height:1px;background:rgb(199 140 255/.35);content:""}.yuga-flow strong{color:#efe3ff;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.76rem;letter-spacing:.02em}.yuga-table{display:grid;margin-top:1.4rem;border:1px solid rgb(199 140 255/.16);border-radius:.85rem;overflow:hidden}.yuga-table article{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(8rem,.8fr) minmax(7rem,.55fr) 1fr;gap:1rem;align-items:center;padding:.85rem 1rem;background:rgb(255 255 255/.018)}.yuga-table article+article{border-top:1px solid rgb(199 140 255/.1)}.yuga-table strong,.yuga-table span,.yuga-table small{display:block}.yuga-table span,.yuga-table small{color:#99938a;font-size:.76rem}.yuga-table strong{font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.1rem;font-weight:500}
  .scale-list{margin-top:1.25rem;border-block:1px solid rgb(199 140 255/.14)}.scale-list div{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.65rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgb(199 140 255/.08)}.scale-list span{color:#aaa49a}.scale-list strong{font-size:.9rem}.number-ladder{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr));gap:.7rem;margin:1.4rem 0}.number-ladder div{border:1px solid rgb(199 140 255/.16);border-radius:.8rem;padding:1rem;background:linear-gradient(145deg,rgb(199 140 255/.08),rgb(255 255 255/.018))}.number-ladder span{color:#c78cff;font-size:.62rem;font-weight:900;letter-spacing:.14em}.number-ladder p{margin:.55rem 0 .25rem;color:#d0c5b3;font-size:.86rem;line-height:1.35}.number-ladder strong{display:block;color:#e3b4ff;font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem}.references{border-bottom:0}
  .full-guide{border:0}.full-guide:not([data-compact])>summary{display:none}.full-guide>summary{display:flex;width:fit-content;align-items:center;gap:.55rem;margin-top:.9rem;border:1px solid rgb(199 140 255/.25);border-radius:999px;padding:.55rem .8rem;background:rgb(199 140 255/.035);color:var(--accent);cursor:pointer;list-style:none}.full-guide>summary::-webkit-details-marker{display:none}.full-guide>summary span{color:#99938a;font-size:.62rem;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase}.full-guide>summary b{font-size:.67rem}.full-guide[open]>summary b{font-size:0}.full-guide[open]>summary b::after{font-size:.67rem;content:"Hide full guide"}.full-guide[open]>.reading-body{animation:guide-reveal 420ms ease both}
  .continue{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;margin-top:2rem;border:1px solid rgb(140 145 255/.24);border-radius:1rem;padding:1.2rem 1.35rem;background:linear-gradient(100deg,rgb(140 145 255/.06),transparent);color:#aeb1ff}.continue span>*{display:block}.continue small{color:#99938a;font-size:.62rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase}.continue strong{font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;font-weight:400}.continue em{color:#aaa49a;font-size:.72rem;font-style:normal}.continue b{font-size:.68rem;text-transform:uppercase}
  .maha-reading{border:1px solid rgb(199 140 255/.2);border-radius:1.4rem;padding-bottom:1rem;background:linear-gradient(145deg,rgb(25 19 40/.72),rgb(3 13 20/.48));box-shadow:inset 0 1px rgb(255 255 255/.035),0 24px 80px rgb(0 0 0/.22);backdrop-filter:blur(20px) saturate(115%)}
  .facts{overflow:hidden;border:1px solid rgb(199 140 255/.2);border-radius:1rem;background:rgb(255 255 255/.025);box-shadow:inset 0 1px rgb(255 255 255/.025);backdrop-filter:blur(14px)}
  .note,.yuga-table article,.number-ladder div,.continue,.full-guide>summary{box-shadow:inset 0 1px rgb(255 255 255/.025);backdrop-filter:blur(14px)}
  @keyframes guide-reveal{from{opacity:0;transform:translateY(-8px)}to{opacity:1;transform:none}}
  @media(max-width:800px){.facts,.yuga-flow,.number-ladder{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr}.yuga-table article{grid-template-columns:1fr}.facts div:nth-child(odd){border-left:0}.facts div:nth-child(n+3){border-top:1px solid rgb(199 140 255/.14)}.continue{align-items:flex-end}}
</style>